        'equivalent event differs by less than the differential splicing threshold with 90% confidence, and an '
        'event cannot be both differentially spliced and equivalent; the analysis was repeated with ΔPSI thresholds'
        ' of 0.05 and 0.2 and with equivalence margins of 0.025 and 0.05 (Table S5). To classify each event in each'
        ' immune cell type between species, while accounting for the differences between datasets of the same '
        'species, the PSI of each junction was averaged in each dataset over the samples with at least 20 JSRs in '
        'the cluster (Figure 1B, C). An event was considered testable in a cell type if every junction of the event '
        'had a PSI in each of the three human and two mouse datasets. A testable event was classified as '
        'differentially spliced between species if, for at least one of its junctions, the PSI of every human '
        'dataset differed from the PSI of every mouse dataset by at least a gap g, that is, the lowest PSI of one '
        'species exceeded the highest PSI of the other species by at least g, so that the human-mouse difference is'
        ' larger than any difference between datasets of the same species; high confidence, g = 0.2; low '
        'confidence, 0.1 ≤ g < 0.2. A testable event was classified as equivalent if, for every junction, the PSI '
        'of all five datasets, human and mouse, lay within a range of at most 0.1; high confidence, at most 0.05; '
        'low confidence, more than 0.05 and at most 0.1. All other testable events were considered inconclusive. To'
        ' estimate the number of events that are separated by chance, that is, by differences between datasets '
        'rather than between species, the gap was calculated in the same way for the nine other ways of splitting '
        'the five datasets into a group of three and a group of two, each of which mixes human and mouse datasets; '
        'the expected number of falsely differentially spliced events was the mean number of events reaching the '
        'gap over these nine splits, and the false discovery rate (FDR) was estimated as the expected divided by '
        'the observed number. This classification is a separation criterion with a permutation-based FDR rather '
        'than a statistical test: no P value was calculated for individual events, because three human and two '
        'mouse datasets can be split into a group of three and a group of two in only ten ways, so that even a '
        'complete separation of the species has a permutation P value of 0.1; the error rate was therefore '
        'estimated for the set of differentially spliced events as a whole. The usage of the minor isoform of an event was defined as the PSI of the less used '
